--TEST--
`Core\Reflect::forClass` reaches the same description by name that `forObject` reaches through a value, and a name nothing declares is `null` — `rule:core-classes/reflect` and `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R6
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Point {
    public int $x = 3;
    public int $y = 4;
    private int $scratch = 0;
}

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R6: absence is `?T`, so a name the program declares no class for is
// `null` rather than a throw — that `null` is the whole of what `class_exists`
// answered, and asking about a name that turns out to be nothing is not a
// mistake in the program.
class Describe {
    public static function of(string $name): void {
        var $info = Core\Reflect::forClass($name);
        if ($info == null) {
            echo $name, " absent\n";
            return;
        }
        echo $info->name(), " ", Core\Arr::count($info->readableProperties()), "\n";
    }
}

Describe::of("Point");
Describe::of("Nowhere");

// `rule:core-classes/reflect`: the two doors are two ways to the same descriptor, so the
// description must not depend on which one was used. Asserted as an agreement
// between them rather than as two right-looking answers.
var $byName = Core\Reflect::forClass("Point");
var $byValue = Core\Reflect::forObject(new Point());
if ($byName == null) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
} else {
    echo $byName->name() == $byValue->name() ? "same class\n" : "different class\n";
    echo Core\Arr::count($byName->readableProperties()) == Core\Arr::count($byValue->readableProperties())
        ? "same properties\n"
        : "different properties\n";
    // § 2's visibility check is the description's, not the door's: the
    // `private` slot is missing from a description reached by name too, and
    // reading it through one is refused on the same terms.
    echo $byName->get(new Point(), "y"), "\n";
    try {
        echo $byName->get(new Point(), "scratch"), "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        echo "private refused\n";
    }
}

// A `Core` class is not among the answers: it declares no properties a walk
// could name, and its surface is its reference cards rather than a
// description.
Describe::of("Core\\Str");
--EXPECT--
Point 2
Nowhere absent
same class
same properties
4
private refused
Core\Str absent
